    <body>
      <sec id="article-25555.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>A nasogastric (NG) tube is a flexible catheter inserted through the nasal passage into the stomach for diagnostic and therapeutic purposes. It is commonly used for gastric decompression in bowel obstruction or ileus, enteral nutrition delivery in patients unable to maintain adequate oral intake, medication administration, and gastric lavage in selected toxicologic emergencies. Proper placement technique, confirmation of intragastric location (preferably via radiographic verification), and vigilant monitoring are essential to minimize complications such as airway misplacement, aspiration, sinusitis, epistaxis, esophageal injury, and, rarely, intracranial placement in patients with basilar skull fractures. Appropriate indication selection and adherence to evidence-based insertion and maintenance protocols are critical to patient safety.</p>
        <p>Clinicians participating in this course enhance their competence in appropriate patient selection, safe insertion techniques, and reliable confirmation of NG tube placement. The activity strengthens understanding of indications and contraindications, and reinforces evidence-based strategies for enteral feeding initiation and gastric decompression. Participants also refine interdisciplinary communication and documentation practices to ensure safe transitions of care and improved patient outcomes in both acute and critical care settings.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Differentiate appropriate clinical scenarios for nasogastric tube use, such as bowel obstruction or enteral feeding support, from situations requiring alternative approaches, including orogastric tubes, post-pyloric feeding tubes, or percutaneous enteral access.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Compare bedside confirmation techniques&#x02014;including pH testing of aspirate and auscultation&#x02014;with radiographic verification, recognizing diagnostic accuracy, safety limitations, and clinical situations in which imaging confirmation is mandatory.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Assess patients for appropriate indications, contraindications, and anatomic or clinical risk factors such as facial trauma, coagulopathy, altered mental status, or suspected basilar skull fracture that may increase complications during nasogastric tube placement.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Strategize with&#x000a0;the interprofessional team to improve care coordination and communication regarding safe nasogastric tube placement.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=25555&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=25555">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25555.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Nasogastric tubes are tubes inserted through a nostril to pass through the pharynx, down the esophagus, and into the stomach (see&#x000a0;<bold>Image.</bold>&#x000a0;Nasogastric Tube). Dr Abraham Levin first described their use in 1921.&#x000a0;Nasogastric tubes are typically used to decompress the stomach in intestinal obstruction or ileus.&#x000a0;Alternatively, they can be used to administer nutrition or medication to patients who cannot swallow or tolerate oral intake, but who have a functioning gastrointestinal tract.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25555.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25555.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Depending on the tube's intended purpose, there are different types, each designed for that purpose. Longer tubes can serve as nasoenteric tubes, with the distal tip in the duodenum or the jejunum, rather than the stomach. Orogastric tubes, placed through the mouth rather than the nose, are used when nasal obstruction is present, in patients receiving mechanical ventilation, and in neonates.&#x000a0;Nasogastric and/or orogastric tubes are usually placed at the bedside with patient cooperation, but fluoroscopy or endoscopy may be used to facilitate the procedure.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25555.s3" sec-type="Anatomy and Physiology">
        <title>Anatomy and Physiology</title>
        <p>Insertion of a nasogastric tube begins at the nares&#x000a0;(nostrils). The nasal sinus connects to the nasopharynx, which is continuous with the oropharynx, 5 to 7 cm posterior to the nares.&#x000a0;The pharynx's length from the skull's base to the start of the esophagus is 12 to 14 cm. The esophagus starts at the upper esophageal sphincter (at the level of the cricopharyngeus) and runs down through the diaphragm to the stomach, measuring approximately 25 cm in length. The lower esophageal sphincter and esophagogastric (or squamocolumnar) junction define the entrance to the stomach.</p>
        <p>While the stomach is a highly distensible structure and, therefore, can vary in length, the empty stomach is generally around 25 cm long. Therefore, to insert a tube through the nares into the stomach's center, about 55 cm of the tube should be inserted.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25555.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;Several methods estimate the depth at which a nasogastric tube should be placed. All estimation methods have some margin of error.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25555.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;A&#x000a0;common preprocedure maneuver is to loop the tube over&#x000a0;1 of the patient's ears, place the tip at the xiphoid process, and use that distance to estimate the length of tube to be inserted.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25555.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>The most proximal portion of the stomach is the cardia. The fundus is the uppermost section of the stomach, situated directly beneath the diaphragm and distal to the cardia.&#x000a0;The largest and most central portion&#x000a0;is the body or corpus of the stomach,&#x000a0;featuring prominent mucosal and submucosal folds (rugae). The antrum is the most distal portion of the stomach, ending at the pyloric channel, which connects&#x000a0;the stomach to the first part of the duodenum.</p>
        <p>Guidance for proper placement of a nasoenteric tube into the duodenum or jejunum is accomplished with fluoroscopy or endoscopy. The duodenum has&#x000a0;4 parts, the first of which is called the duodenal bulb. The second part is also called the descending part; the third part is the transverse part; and the fourth part is the ascending part that connects at the Ligament of Treitz to the jejunum.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25555.s4" sec-type="Indications">
        <title>Indications</title>
        <p>Many indications necessitate the insertion of a&#x000a0;nasogastric tube or nasoenteric tube.&#x000a0;A&#x000a0;common indication for placing a&#x000a0;nasogastric tube is to decompress the stomach in the setting of an ileus or a small bowel obstruction.&#x000a0;Intractable nausea or emesis, whether caused by medications, intoxication, or other reasons, can be an indication for the placement of a nasogastric tube to prevent aspiration. Prophylactic placement of nasogastric tubes in patients undergoing abdominal surgery is not recommended.&#x000a0;In fact, patients who develop a postoperative ileus tend to recover faster without the placement of a nasogastric tube.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25555.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;Indications include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Decompression of the stomach
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>This procedure is seen in the setting of obstruction at the pylorus and distally, or with a postoperative or other cause of ileus. Small bowel obstruction from adhesions, hernias, obstructing neoplasms, volvulus, intussusception, and many other causes may block the normal passage of bodily fluids, including salivary, gastric, hepatobiliary, and enteric secretions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25555.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;These fluids accumulate, leading to abdominal distension, pain, and nausea. Eventually, the fluids build up enough that nausea progresses to emesis, putting the patient at risk for aspiration and pneumonia, an event with mortality as high as 70%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25555.r7">[7]</xref>&#x000a0;Nasogastric tube placement is considered first-line therapy&#x000a0;for postoperative anastomotic obstruction and leakage,&#x000a0;but it is not recommended for prophylactic use after abdominal or thoracic surgery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25555.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25555.r8">[8]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Delivery of nutrition and/or medicine
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Diagnosis and careful case-by-case evaluation play a key role in decision-making. Clinicians must determine whether a patient has a functional gastrointestinal tract and can willfully consume solids and liquids without resulting complications. Before placement of an enteral feeding tube, it must be determined that the patient cannot or will not take adequate nutrition orally and that there is no intestinal obstruction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25555.r9">[9]</xref> Post-pyloric feeding should be selected in patients with recurrent vomiting, a history of gastroesophageal aspiration, or if the patient has gastroparesis. Typical situations where&#x000a0;tube feedings are indicated include the following:
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Unconscious or mentally incapacitated patients
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Head injury, endotracheal intubation</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item><list-item><p>Swallowing disorders
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Stroke (within the first week after the event), multiple sclerosis, motor neuron disease (eg, amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, progressive bulbar or pseudobulbar palsy), Huntington disease, head and neck cancer, esophageal dysmotility, partial esophageal obstruction</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item><list-item><p>Nasogastric tubes may be placed for nutritional support while waiting to see how much function the patient recovers after a stroke or during and after treatment, such as radiation therapy, that may affect the upper aerodigestive tract. If the patient does not recover their swallowing ability or requires long-term nutritional support, a more permanent feeding tube, such as a gastrostomy or jejunostomy, should be placed.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Physiological anorexia, such as with liver disease</p></list-item><list-item><p>Partial intestinal failure
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Inflammatory bowel disease, short bowel syndrome</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item><list-item><p>Increased nutritional requirements
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Cystic fibrosis, renal disease</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item><list-item><p>Psychological problems
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Anorexia nervosa, severe depression</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item><list-item><p>Premature infants</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Poison removal, or&#x000a0;in the setting of an overdose of an orally ingested agent or agents
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>These problems can also result in intractable nausea, placing the patient at aspiration risk. Placing a nasogastric tube can reduce this risk.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Diagnosis and management of upper gastrointestinal bleeds
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Nasogastric tube placement in this setting can determine if there is blood in the stomach and whether there is active bleeding.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25555.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;Recovery of red blood that does not clear with irrigation of the tube strongly suggests that there is active upper gastrointestinal bleeding, and is an indication for prompt endoscopic examination.</p></list-item><list-item><p>The tube is then used to irrigate and to suction blood and clots from the stomach before endoscopy, improving visualization of the mucosa that would otherwise be obscured by blood.&#x000a0;Red blood in the tube that clears with irrigation is compatible with either a minimal upper gastrointestinal bleed or with blood from the nose or pharynx induced by tube placement.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Coffee-ground material in the stomach usually represents "old blood" from inactive bleeding. An active upper gastrointestinal source of bleeding is only ruled out if the fluid aspirated from the stomach is bile-tinged. If the&#x000a0;aspirate is colorless (not bile-tinged), it is still possible that a duodenal ulcer or some other cause of duodenal bleeding may be present, but blood has not refluxed into the stomach.&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p>A duodenal ulcer may&#x000a0;cause bleeding but also scar the pylorus, leading to partial gastric outlet obstruction and&#x000a0;preventing reflux of blood&#x000a0;into the stomach.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25555.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;Placement of a&#x000a0;nasogastric tube thus helps manage upper gastrointestinal bleeding, but it has not been shown to improve patient outcomes&#x000a0;in this setting.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25555.r11">[11]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25555.s5" sec-type="Contraindications">
        <title>Contraindications</title>
        <p>Absolute&#x000a0;contraindications of&#x000a0;nasogastric tube placement:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Inability to obtain informed&#x000a0;consent</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Procedural refusal</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Severe craniofacial trauma
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Nasogastric tube&#x000a0;placement without fluoroscopic guidance may&#x000a0;result in inadvertent intracranial placement, with catastrophic consequences.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25555.r12">[12]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Gastrointestinal tract obstruction
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>This is an absolute contraindication to enteral tube feeding.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Relative contraindications:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Hemodynamic instability</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Uncorrected intrinsic coagulation abnormality or anticoagulant use</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Esophageal varices, in which case tube placement could cause life-threatening hemorrhage</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Placement soon after banding of esophageal varices</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Severe gastroparesis. When tube feedings are necessary in such patients, a nasoenteric tube is more effective.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>After Roux-en-Y gastric bypass surgery,&#x000a0;partial gastrectomy, or other bariatric surgery
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>A nasoenteric tube may be appropriate, but&#x000a0;nasogastric tube placement may not be suitable or feasible.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25555.r13">[13]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Esophageal trauma, especially in the setting of ingestion of caustic substances, where the placement of a nasogastric tube may create or worsen perforations</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25555.s6" sec-type="Equipment">
        <title>Equipment</title>
        <p>In addition to the tube, the following items should be available at the bedside before&#x000a0;nasogastric tube placement: a water-based lubricant that may or may not contain lidocaine, a large (usually 60 mL) syringe, and tape or a tube fixation device.&#x000a0;Most&#x000a0;nasogastric tubes have markings indicating how far the tube has been passed, which facilitates proper placement. Conventionally, a tube is advanced about 55 cm from the nares to reach&#x000a0;the stomach in an adult, although this distance varies.</p>
        <p>Several different types of&#x000a0;nasogastric tubes exist and can be used depending on the purpose of the insertion. All of these types have a radiopaque line or markings to confirm the tubes' correct placement.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>A Salem Sump tube made of polyvinylchloride is relatively stiff and difficult to tolerate for prolonged use, but it is&#x000a0;the most commonly used tube for gastric decompression.&#x000a0;This tube has 2 lumens:&#x000a0;1 for suction or injection of air, fluids, medications, or nutrition, and the other is open to the air to minimize suctioning of gastric mucosa into the tube. The most common sizes are 14 and 18 F, but smaller sizes are available.&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The Levin tube is a single-lumen nasogastric tube with a side port for drainage near the&#x000a0;distal&#x000a0;tip. This tube can be used for suction and irrigation, but it is probably most suitable for administering nutrition and medication.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>A Dobhoff tube is a soft, small-caliber tube with a weighted distal end that allows it to more easily advance across the pyloric sphincter into the small intestine, taking advantage of antegrade peristalsis rather than requiring manual manipulation.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Enteral feeding tubes, including those produced by Abbott Nutrition, Cook Endoscopy (a nasojejunal tube), Teleflex Medical, Corpak Medical Systems, and Covidien.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Fluoroscopy or endoscopy-guided tube placement is beneficial in cases of difficult or complex anatomy, such as in patients with advanced head and neck cancers, esophageal strictures, severe burns, and after surgical revision of the upper gastrointestinal tract (including head and neck surgery, esophageal reconstruction, hiatal hernia repair, gastric bypass, and gastrectomy).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25555.r14">[14]</xref> The equipment used for endoscopy is beyond the scope of this activity.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Fluoroscopy</bold>
</p>
        <p>Fluoroscopic equipment can be either fixed or mobile. A fixed system uses a radiolucent exam table with an under-table-mounted tube and an imaging detector mounted above the table.&#x000a0;A fixed fluoroscopic&#x000a0;system is most&#x000a0;often used for tube insertion when fluoroscopy is needed. A water-soluble contrast medium, such as gastrograffin, is used to outline the digestive tract as the tube is advanced.</p>
        <p>In cases of complex anatomy, a guidewire may be used to&#x000a0;direct the tube&#x000a0;in the desired direction.&#x000a0;Mobile fluoroscopy is a C-arm, which is a unit that consists of an x-ray source at one end and an image detector at the other.&#x000a0;This device allows the operator greater flexibility and a wider choice of locations. Most&#x000a0;nasogastric or nasoenteric tubes have a radioopaque line along their length or at their tip to visualize their course. Barium or a water-soluble contrast agent, such as gastrograffin, is used in some cases.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Endoscopy</bold>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>This procedure facilitates tube placement in specific settings:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Repeated unsuccessful attempts at blind&#x000a0;nasogastric tube placement</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>High risk of complications from blind tube placement (eg, severe esophagitis, caustic injury, or tight esophageal strictures)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Suspected or known esophageal obstruction due to strictures, rings, neoplasms, or radiation effects</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Altered upper gastrointestinal anatomy (postoperative changes after head and neck, esophageal surgery, or complex foregut surgery)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Need for postpyloric feeding when bedside placement and radiologic guidance are unsuccessful or not feasible</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25555.s7" sec-type="Personnel">
        <title>Personnel</title>
        <p>A nasogastric tube can be placed by a surgeon, registered nurse, nurse practitioner, physician's assistant, or radiologist. Proper evaluation and ongoing treatment of patients with a&#x000a0;nasogastric or nasoenteric tube include a collaborative team of healthcare professionals. Speech and language therapists assist in the initial assessment of the patient, evaluating the individual's physical and mental capacity, and participate in the decision-making process to determine the necessity and safety of the procedure. They will continue to monitor each patient's progress and determine whether and when an enteric tube is no longer needed. If fluoroscopy is required, the tube is usually inserted by a radiologist. If endoscopy is necessary, a team is involved in this process.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25555.s8" sec-type="Preparation">
        <title>Preparation</title>
        <p>The indication for the procedure, potential complications, and treatment alternatives should be explained to the patient.&#x000a0;If the procedure is scheduled, the patient should ideally be made&#x000a0;nil per os for up to 12 hours before tube placement to reduce the risk of vomiting or aspiration.&#x000a0;The patient should be seated, if possible, or in the left lateral decubitus position if necessary. A protective sheet should be placed on the patient's chest. If the tube is being placed for suction, suction tubing and a suction apparatus should be in the room, ready for use immediately after tube placement.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25555.s9" sec-type="Technique or Treatment">
        <title>Technique or Treatment</title>
        <p>The patient is generally placed in the&#x000a0;left lateral decubitus&#x000a0;position or in an upright, seated position. Ideally, the patient should be awake and alert to swallow as the tube is passed. Still, they can be sedated if they are receiving mechanical ventilation or if sedation is required for any other reason.&#x000a0;Both nostrils should be examined to identify any septal deviation and, if so, the tube should be passed into the nostril that is&#x000a0;more widely patent.</p>
        <p>Nasogastric tube placement in an awake, alert patient is usually performed with the patient seated and the neck flexed, if possible. A straw is placed in the patient's mouth, and they are encouraged to swallow water as the tube is placed. Applying 2% topical lidocaine gel to the distal tip of the tube is recommended for local anesthesia and to lubricate the passage through the nasopharynx. A guidewire is used with a thinner, more flexible tube to guide it into the esophagus. The lubricated tip of the tube is first advanced parallel to the ground toward the nasopharynx, then&#x000a0;further to the stomach or small bowel.</p>
        <p>Fluoroscopy is used in some cases to provide real-time visualization as the tube is placed, confirming that its tip is in the desired location.&#x000a0;In intubated individuals, performing a reverse Sellick maneuver (pulling the thyroid cartilage up rather than pushing it down during intubation) and freezing the&#x000a0;nasogastric tube may facilitate tube placement.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25555.r15">[15]</xref>&#x000a0;Once the tube has been inserted appropriately, typically to approximately 55 cm, as previously noted, it should be secured to the patient's nose with tape. If feeding is planned through the tube, then it is imperative to confirm its location, as placing feeds into the lungs can cause potentially fatal complications.</p>
        <p>The ideal location for a nasogastric tube placed for suction is in the body or antrum of the stomach. After placement of a decompressive tube, the tube is attached to extension tubing and to a suction machine set to intermittent or continuous suction.&#x000a0;Confirmation of placement of a larger-caliber tube, such as a Salem sump or a single- or double-lumen Levin tube, in the stomach&#x000a0;should be confirmed by aspirating bilious (yellow or green) gastric fluid with a large (usually 60 mL) syringe. If too little fluid is returned or none at all, the tube should be repositioned and the same step repeated. If&#x000a0;bilious fluid&#x000a0;cannot be withdrawn through the tube, an x-ray should be performed to assess the tube's position.</p>
        <p>The correct placement of thinner or more flexible tubes should always be confirmed with a plain chest or upper abdominal x-ray.&#x000a0;Pushing air through the tube with a large syringe while auscultating the epigastrium with a stethoscope is a commonly described maneuver for determining the tube's location. Still, it is of dubious value and should not be used.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25555.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25555.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;Misplaced&#x000a0;nasogastric tubes in the left mainstem and small bowel can sound similar to those in an adequately placed position.&#x000a0;When the tube is no longer needed, it should be removed slowly, as forceful removal can lead to knotting.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25555.r18">[18]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25555.s10" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Complications of placement and maintenance of a&#x000a0;nasogastric tube include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Pain or epistaxis during tube placement typically resolves spontaneously over time or upon tube removal.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Tube placement may cause or worsen a perforation in the setting of esophageal trauma, particularly after caustic ingestion.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Blind placement of the tube in patients with injury to the cribriform plate may result in intracranial tube placement.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25555.r19">[19]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Misplacement of a tube into the airway, then introducing medication or tube feeds to the lungs, can cause aspiration&#x000a0;pneumonia, pneumothorax, pleural effusion, hydrothorax, tissue damage in the lung, septic shock, respiratory failure, and death.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25555.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;This is a particular hazard in intubated patients or other individuals who lack an effective gag reflex, in whom a nasogastric tube can be accidentally placed into the airway without symptoms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25555.r20">[20]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Larger-diameter tubes may keep the lower and upper esophageal sphincters open while in place. If the tube becomes obstructed or malfunctions and cannot decompress the stomach, it may increase the risk of aspiration due to the stenting effect.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25555.r21">[21]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Prolonged use of&#x000a0;nasogastric tubes can irritate the gastric lining, causing bleeding.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25555.r22">[22]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Patients with extensive irrigation with a nasogastric tube can develop electrolyte abnormalities such as hypokalemia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25555.r23">[23]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Prolonged pressure against the nostril can cause pressure ulceration or necrosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25555.r24">[24]</xref>&#x000a0;The tube should be retaped intermittently to prevent this complication.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25555.s11" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>Whether decompressing the stomach, providing enteral access for nutrition and medications in a patient unable to tolerate them orally, or in the setting of an acute gastrointestinal bleed, nasogastric tubes are part of the standard of care for many routine health issues. Physicians and other care providers should be able to place a nasogastric tube when indicated and manage them effectively and readily. Given the potential for significant complications, particularly when tube placement is misdirected or inappropriate, the entire healthcare team must understand the indications, contraindications, potential complications, and appropriate evaluation to confirm proper tube placement.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25555.s12" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Safe and effective nasogastric tube management requires technical proficiency, clinical judgment, and structured team-based coordination. Physicians and advanced practitioners must apply appropriate patient selection criteria, recognize contraindications (eg, suspected basilar skull fracture, severe facial trauma), and ensure evidence-based confirmation of tube placement, preferably radiographic verification, prior to use for feeding or medication administration. Strategic decision-making includes selecting the appropriate tube type and size, determining the indication (decompression vs enteral nutrition), and establishing clear orders for suction settings or feeding protocols. Early recognition of complications such as misplacement, aspiration, epistaxis, sinusitis, or electrolyte abnormalities is critical to patient safety.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25555.r25">[25]</xref></p>
        <p>Nurses play a central role in insertion (where within scope), bedside verification per institutional protocol, securement, monitoring tube position, maintaining patency, documenting output, and providing patient education. Pharmacists contribute by evaluating the suitability of medications for enteral administration, preventing tube occlusion, adjusting formulations, and monitoring for drug&#x02013;nutrient interactions. Dietitians optimize enteral nutrition plans and caloric targets. Clear interprofessional communication, particularly during handoffs and transitions of care, ensures verification status, feeding regimens, and tube care instructions are consistently conveyed. Coordinated, standardized protocols enhance team performance, reduce preventable adverse events, and promote patient-centered, safe use of nasogastric tubes&#x000a0;across acute and critical care settings.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25555.s13">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
